{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794552595,"nonce":"/lgrssi97R0A+7EM76GTKRcFf94=","message":"Threads in an Ash side module, continued (2026-11-13). Ash's session (ash-13) traced the allocation failure: it was hl_abi::ProgramAllocator returning null for any alignment above 16, which hxn-future's worker pool needs (64-aligned cache-padded values), not thread setup; Ash supports a module starting its own threads (wasi thread-spawn; the host gives each module thread a TLS block). Its fix, over-allocating and keeping the block's address below the aligned pointer, is committed in hl_abi as 049508e (not pushed yet), with a test of alignments 1..4096 and realloc that passes natively and under Miri. It also closes the 'over-aligned handles' gap above.\n\nWith it, tests/counter/run.sh wasm passes as a wasm32-wasip1-threads side module (+atomics,+bulk-memory, --shared-memory, the module's own #[thread_local] errno), worker pool and panic included. gen-wasm aborts on the module's second thread with 'current thread handle already set during thread spawn': the module first spawns after a panic printed on the main thread (which sets std's CURRENT there), and the new thread appears to get a copy of the main thread's live TLS rather than the module's initial image. Reported to ash-13. Threaded side modules stay unsupported in hxn until Ash fixes that; nothing of it is committed in hxn.","files":null}]}